I understand... There were two mistakes:

- knit_expand does not "knit": it only replaces the delimited variables; thus the output of knit_expand is a Rnw file, not a tex file; and when \Sexpr{paste(knit(text = out), collapse = '\n')} is executed in the first section, value is 2 (the last value of the loop) !
- knit_child "knits": it compiles the R code and then generates at tex file; therefore in my second section, \Sexpr{paste(knit(text = out), collapse = '\n')} is totally useless, because "out" is some pure LaTeX code, without R code, and there's nothing to "knit" !

This is the correct way:

\section{Using knit\_expand}

<<>>=
out <- NULL
for(value in 1:2){

    out <- c(out, knit(text = knit_expand("child.Rnw"), quiet=TRUE))
}
@

\Sexpr{paste(out, collapse = '\n')}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Using knit\_child}

<<>>=
out <- NULL
for(value in 1:2){
    out <- c(out, knit_child("child.Rnw", quiet=TRUE))
}
@

\Sexpr{paste(out, collapse = '\n')}

